<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Ciarraige Luachra</h1><p class="st-meta">450 CE – 1200 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">North Kerry kingdom of the Ciarraige, the people who gave County Kerry its name, later ruled by the O'Connor Kerry family.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Ciarraige, 'people of Ciar', were one of several groups of that name in early Ireland, claiming descent from a legendary ancestor Ciar. The Ciarraige Luachra held north Kerry around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tralee">Tralee</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-feale">Feale</a> valley from the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a>, as a subject people paying tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eoganachta">Eóganachta</a> kings of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-munster">Kingdom of Munster</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Their kingdom was a land of bog, upland and coastal plain, and its wealth lay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>. St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brendan-the-navigator">Brendan the Navigator</a>, founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clonfert">Clonfert</a>, was traditionally born in their territory near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tralee">Tralee</a>, and the region had a lively church life. Their kings, from the 11th century known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oconnor-kerry">Ó Conchobhair Chiarraí</a>, O'Connor Kerry, held a respected place among the lesser kings of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-munster">Munster</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 13th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-normans">Anglo-Norman</a> lords, especially the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fitzgerald-dynasty">FitzGeralds</a>, took much of their land, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oconnor-kerry">O'Connors Kerry</a> were confined to the north of the county, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraghticonnor">Iraghticonnor</a>, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrigafoyle-castle">Carrigafoyle Castle</a> until the late 16th century. The people's name lives on in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-kerry">County Kerry</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 450 CE – 1200 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">British Isles</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:68.66%;width:14.93%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>450 CE – 1200 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Irish, later Middle Irish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ciarraige, Kerry</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=825&amp;focus=ciarraige-luachra" aria-label="Ciarraige Luachra on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4512 358 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ciarraige Luachra lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4823 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4832 614h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4832 614h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Ireland. <a href="/atlas?year=825&amp;focus=ciarraige-luachra">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

